# Line endings. Text is LF in the repository and in the working tree on every
# platform, with two sets of exceptions below.
#
# This used to be a deliberately narrow file that pinned only the shell scripts,
# to avoid renormalising the repository. The cost of leaving it narrow was 26
# files committed with CRLF - 22 of them Python - which is how a four-line edit
# to PORTING-NOTES.md once produced a 1346-line diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# A shell script with CRLF endings fails outright on Linux and macOS: the kernel
# reads the shebang as `/bin/bash` and reports "bad interpreter". Git on Windows
# will happily hand out CRLF unless told otherwise.
packaging/AppRun       text eol=lf
packaging/build_linux  text eol=lf
packaging/build_mac    text eol=lf
packaging/build_win    text eol=lf
*.sh                   text eol=lf

# The Windows shells want the opposite.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never translate these. `text=auto` decides from content, and a PDF is
# text-shaped enough to be guessed wrong - an earlier attempt at renormalising
# this repository corrupted the pdf and png files. 47 of the PDFs matched here
# are the test suite's fixtures, so a wrong guess breaks the tests.
#
# Last matching rule wins, so these must stay below `* text=auto`.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.mo    binary
